\f1\fs22 Agent Jones knew the mortuary\'92s reputation\'97little more than a resource tap for the teaching hospital next door. The joint paid reasonably well for fresh bodies, which kept the place busy with a steady conga line of ambulances piloted by bent orderlies. Whoever carted in Emil\'92s corpse must\'92ve needed the extra cash. The mortuary was in a different security zone from both Emil\'92s place and Travis\'92s office. Crossing that line usually meant haggling the price with border grubs, hardly worth the hassle.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Jones arrived at the mortuary just on dusk. Emil was laid out on a stainless steel slab, one among dozens in the main necropsy hall. It was a busy scene: rows of pathologists murmuring quietly into ceiling mics while they sawed and sloshed through bone and meat. Jars clinked as organs and samples splashed in, watched by med students fighting their gag reflexes. A few sick units giggled now and then.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I will see his effects,\'94 Jones told a nurse in a medical gown. She nodded, led him to a row of big, gulping cabinets, and retrieved a crumpled plastic bag. Jones tipped it out on a table: midnight-blue suit, shiny new shoes, novelty socks. He patted down the suit, rifled the pockets, dug around in the soles, and turned out every seam.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You sure this is everything?\'94 he asked, straight-faced.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That\'92s everything, sir.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The security footage, please.\'94 Jones scrolled through the feed, but found no sign of the disc. It had never made it in.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He skimmed the delivery record. \'93The ambos have the disc,\'94 he muttered, already picturing a house call or two.
